## When This Isn't the Right Fit

If your night eating feels out of your control more often than not, or it's tied up with something heavier, an app timer isn't the right size of help. A doctor, dietitian or counselor is. We make a small tool, we don't give health advice, and we won't pretend otherwise.

It also won't help if what you want is a multi-week program with lessons and check-ins. Pick a tracker or a coaching service for that.

And if you've set a rule like "no eating after 7pm" and you're wondering what it does to the body, that's a question for a health professional, not an app. What we can speak to is the practical side: a rule is easy at 3pm and wobbly at 11pm, and that wobble is the moment a short pause is for.

One honest note. This won't work every time. Some nights you'll wait the three minutes and still eat the thing. The pause still counted.

## FAQ

### Is there an app or timer that helps stop impulsive eating at night?

Yes, in a few forms. Habit trackers help you see patterns, blockers limit other apps, and short timer tools like Not This Time give you a three-minute pause at the moment of the urge. Which one fits depends on whether you need a look-back or a pause.

### Can I just use my phone's built-in timer?

You can, and for some people that's enough. A plain timer gives you the delay but nothing to do while it runs. Not This Time adds a ticking sound and audio over the three minutes so the wait has some company.

### What happens when you stop eating after 7pm?

We can't speak to what it does in your body. That's a question for a doctor or dietitian. On the practical side, a cutoff like that is easier to keep when you have a small plan for the moment it gets tested, like a three-minute pause.

Next time you're hunting for an app or timer that helps stop impulsive eating at night, start with the three minutes in front of you.

